/// A module that DECLARES a leaf and also IMPORTS a module declaring the same
/// leaf -- the tier-2/tier-3 boundary of spec §4, which nothing else in this
/// corpus reaches.
///
/// Every other collision here is import-vs-import (`two_in_scope`) or
/// consumer-vs-its-import (`uses_red`). Both live entirely in tier 3, so they
/// exercise rule 2 (ambiguity WITHIN a tier) and never rule 1 (an inner tier
/// shadows an outer one). Rule 1 is the one §4 is actually about:
///
///     locals / generic params
///       -> current-module items          <- THIS module declares `tint`/`Paint`
///       -> imported modules' export sets <- ...and imports Red's
///       -> prelude
///
/// So `tint` and `Paint` written bare in this file mean THIS module's, and
/// `ResolutionScope::Red` being in scope is irrelevant to that. A module must
/// be able to name its own declarations.
///
/// The import is written BEFORE the declarations. `owns_after.cryo` is the
/// same file with the import moved below them, and the two must agree: source
/// order is not part of what a name means. They are separate modules rather
/// than one file compiled twice because module discovery is import-driven --
/// a file nothing imports is never compiled at all.
///
/// Values are distinct from Red's (1) so a wrong bind returns a wrong NUMBER
/// rather than a compile error, per this project's design rule.
![config(testing)]
namespace ResolutionScope::OwnsBefore;

import ResolutionScope::Red;

function tint() -> i32 { return 7; }

type struct Paint {
    v: i32;

    // Fully qualified on purpose: the type's own signature must not depend on
    // the thing under test, or a wrong bind here would mask the wrong bind
    // in the annotation positions below.
    static mix() -> ResolutionScope::OwnsBefore::Paint {
        return ResolutionScope::OwnsBefore::Paint { v: 7 };
    }
    shade(&this) -> i32 { return this.v; }
}

/// VALUE LANE. Bare `tint()` with Red's `tint` also in scope.
function own_bare_call() -> i32 { return tint(); }

/// TYPE LANE, return position.
function own_bare_return() -> Paint { return ResolutionScope::OwnsBefore::Paint::mix(); }

/// TYPE LANE, parameter position. Called with this module's own `Paint`, so a
/// bind to `Red::Paint` is a hard error rather than a wrong number.
function own_bare_param(p: Paint) -> i32 { return p.shade(); }

/// TYPE LANE, body-local declaration -- the position that compiles, links and
/// returns the wrong number with no diagnostic at all.
function own_bare_local() -> i32 {
    mut a: Paint = ResolutionScope::OwnsBefore::Paint::mix();
    return a.shade();
}

/// TYPE LANE, struct field.
type struct Holder {
    p: Paint;

    static wrap() -> ResolutionScope::OwnsBefore::Holder {
        return ResolutionScope::OwnsBefore::Holder {
            p: ResolutionScope::OwnsBefore::Paint::mix()
        };
    }
    unwrap(&this) -> i32 { return this.p.shade(); }
}

function own_bare_field() -> i32 {
    const h: ResolutionScope::OwnsBefore::Holder =
        ResolutionScope::OwnsBefore::Holder::wrap();
    return h.unwrap();
}

/// TYPE LANE, bare return annotation on a STATIC METHOD -- the fifth
/// annotation position, and the only one the types above cannot cover: their
/// own `mix()` is qualified on purpose, so that a wrong bind in the type's own
/// signature cannot mask the wrong bind under test elsewhere. This type exists
/// to put that position under test in isolation, and nothing else uses it.
type struct Tin {
    v: i32;

    static fill() -> Paint {                       // <- bare, on a static method
        return ResolutionScope::OwnsBefore::Paint::mix();
    }
}

function own_bare_static_return() -> i32 {
    return ResolutionScope::OwnsBefore::Tin::fill().shade();
}

/// EXPRESSION position, as the control on all of the above.
///
/// `Paint::mix()` here is a path EXPRESSION, not an annotation. Measured
/// before the fix: with every annotation in the file qualified so nothing can
/// leak into it, this position bound correctly while all five annotation
/// positions bound to the import. That asymmetry is spec §2.6, and the fix
/// must not "repair" it by changing this side -- a regression here is as much
/// a failure as one above.
function own_expr_position() -> i32 { return Paint::mix().shade(); }

/// The import must still WORK: shadowing is not walling-off. `Red` is in this
/// module's scope and its qualified path must resolve to Red's, not to ours.
function the_import_still_resolves() -> i32 { return ResolutionScope::Red::tint(); }

/// The same control for the TYPE lane, and it is NOT redundant with the
/// function one above -- it is the control this fix most needs.
///
/// Shadowing changes HOW a qualified shadowed type is found.
/// `resolve_type_qualified_name_from` resolves the written path by extracting
/// the leaf, resolving it in scope, and accepting the result only when
/// `qualifier_agrees`. Before the fix, bare `Paint` here resolved to
/// `Red::Paint`, the qualifier agreed, and the path was answered by that
/// route. After it, bare `Paint` resolves to THIS module's, the qualifier
/// disagrees, and the answer comes from `resolve_qualified_type_via_exports`
/// -- the M1 export scan -- instead.
///
/// So the fix moves qualified shadowed types onto a mechanism that
/// `docs/name-resolution.md` §6 schedules for DELETION. Deleting it without
/// this control in place makes `Red::Paint` stop resolving from here, and the
/// pressure at that point is to put the deleted mechanism back. The control
/// is written now, while the reason is fresh, so that a future deletion has
/// to supply a replacement rather than a revert.
function the_imported_TYPE_still_resolves() -> i32 {
    const p: ResolutionScope::Red::Paint = ResolutionScope::Red::Paint::mix();
    return p.shade();
}
